## Recommended learning resources

- [The Rust Programming Language (The Book)](https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/?utm_source=chatgpt.com) — the official Rust book and the best place to learn the language.
- [Rust by Example](https://doc.rust-lang.org/rust-by-example/?utm_source=chatgpt.com) — learn by working through short examples.
- [Axum Documentation](https://docs.rs/axum/latest/axum/?utm_source=chatgpt.com) — the official guide for the web framework you'll use.
- [Tokio Documentation](https://tokio.rs/?utm_source=chatgpt.com) — learn asynchronous programming.
- [SQLx Documentation](https://github.com/launchbadge/sqlx?utm_source=chatgpt.com) — compile-time checked SQL queries.
- [The Rust API Guidelines](https://rust-lang.github.io/api-guidelines/?utm_source=chatgpt.com) — conventions for writing idiomatic Rust libraries.

### A capstone project idea

Having previously worked on an event management and ticketing system design, a great capstone would be to implement it in Rust. I will attempt to build an **Event Management API** with:

- User registration and login
- Event creation and management
- Ticket booking
- QR-code ticket generation
- PostgreSQL persistence
- JWT authentication
- Email notifications
- Dockerized deployment
- CI with GitHub Actions

That project will at the end, not only showcase Rust proficiency but also backend architecture, authentication, database design, and deployment—making
